WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:08.150
It's been 150 years since the first
edition of Nature was published in 1869.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:13.559
In that time the science contained
inside the journal has changed a lot but

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:19.800
so has the front cover. I'm Kelly Krause
Creative Director of Nature and I've

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.820
come to the Nature archives to discover
some front covers from Nature's long

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:32.390
history, and to see how the design of the
magazine has changed over the years. Wow

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:38.129
Right well we have all the Nature right
from the first issue on the 4th of

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:43.950
November 1869 right up to the
present-day. So I'm interested in looking

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:49.710
at the covers over time and how they've
changed from being mostly advertisements

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.080
on the cover to being very rich with
imagery.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.840
Part of my job at Nature is to

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.010
design the cover each week. But when the
journal first started the front cover

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.870
was completely different to anything we'd
do today. In fact for nearly 100 years

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.770
the front page was just used for
advertising. You've got this which is

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:13.409
more like announcements about
events more and then you get the instruments

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:14.800
that come on the front.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.360
This is one of my
favorite covers from this era an

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.810
advertisement for buying rock and fossil
collections, and there's one here for a

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.909
sunshine recorder. It really shows how
for a long time science was in many ways

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:31.979
a hobby or a gentleman's pursuit. Looking
through the archived volumes we

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:36.570
eventually found some covers with actual
science on them, but not until the 1960s,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:41.360
and even then it was still only text at
that point. It wasn't until a few years

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.080
later that we started seeing figures and
black-and-white photography.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.780
It's so interesting that in this cover in 1967
the only hint content is this, the text

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.120
here that says "Structure of Jupiter" and
then by 1974 we finally moved to

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.450
actually visualizing the content as the
main event on the cover.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.770
Finally as we

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.979
flicked through the books we started
seeing not just black and white but

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:09.450
full-color images filling the pages and
I particularly appreciated the very

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.140
of their time gradients that became
popular.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.550
So this is in the mid-80s when

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:19.620
gradients are really just kind of
starting. You know, gradients, cutouts, these

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:26.020
covers were still being physically put
together with tape.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.780
Before desktop publishing all of these designs had to
be made on paper. Photos were cut out and

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.280
pasted down and gradients were made by
airbrushing with paint.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.540
After being taped together the final
cover was essentially photographed and

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.220
the photograph sent to print.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.880
By the 90s Nature had embraced desktop publishing

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.070
doing most of the work on computers
using software like Photoshop. There's a

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.820
lot more stuff you can do with an image
once it's digital although some tweaks

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.080
are a little more obvious in hindsight.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.220
And this is a classic from the beginning

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.960
of the desktop publishing era and this
one is one of my particular favorites

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:11.850
because it shows a bit of Photoshop
malfeasance. This is in 1997 and this is

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:17.940
Dolly the sheep and this cover is
amazing because it really speaks volumes

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.830
about the era in which it was made.
If you look closely Dolly you'll see

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.050
that this foot here looks a bit
strange, and the reason is is because

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:35.820
that's actually not Dolly's foot at all
this was cut out in Photoshop and if you

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.630
look at the original picture you'll see
that it's Dolly's mother standing behind

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.770
it became fashionable to do these sort
of cutouts that were more intricate than

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.720
can be done in paste-up so in this she
has a foot that's not her own which is

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.040
fantastic.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.190
When I joined Nature I was
given the opportunity to refine the

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:57.390
cover design for the modern digital era.
This meant aligning our print and web

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:02.130
design but also as Nature's first
creative director making sure that all

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:06.720
covers were evaluated for their artistic
merit, as well as the scientific value of

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.580
the research they showed.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:15.980
I started at Nature in 2011 so I'm just looking at

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:21.990
some things from that era and I've
landed on actually one of my favorite

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:27.390
covers from the very beginning when I
started, which is looking at insect wings.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:33.150
I remember the authors of the paper
submitted about 20 different insects and

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.880
this particular one caught my eye
because it was so unusual. I think it is

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.870
a beautiful photograph but it also says
a lot about the era you know it's still

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.340
a Photoshop cutout but it's meant to
look much more natural like it's in a

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.950
studio, it's meant to give you the sense
that you're just looking at the object

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.360
without any decoration really.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.180
What they don't yet have here in the archives of

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.370
course is our latest cover for Nature's
one hundred and fiftieth anniversary

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:06.150
we've collaborated with a team of
network scientists to produce a graphic

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:11.310
representation of almost every paper
Nature has published since 1900 and I

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:15.660
think it's pretty amazing having visited
the archives and seen the bound volumes

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.290
of these papers to then see them
represented like this on the cover. And

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:24.390
this cover isn't just a static image, we
have also made a video to show the links

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.110
between some of the most important
papers in Nature's history, and an

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.440
interactive experience so anyone can
look through the model and navigate

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.120
their own way between different points.
So this is much more than just a print

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.600
cover. I think it's a great way to 
mark our anniversary.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.340
But perhaps in a hundred and fifty years
some future Creative Director of Nature

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.060
will be here in the archives looking
over our interactive cover and

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:56.209
commenting on how it's very retro and
representative of its time.

